<Base title="How we know it matches what you meant">
    <PageHeader num="13" title="How we know it matches what you meant" lede="A from-scratch regex engine is only as good as its differential testing. This one is validated by the hundreds of millions of cases it doesn't diverge on." />

    <div class="prose prose-zinc max-w-none prose-headings:font-bold prose-a:text-teal-700 prose-code:before:content-none prose-code:after:content-none prose-code:rounded prose-code:bg-zinc-100 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:py-0.5 prose-code:text-[0.85em] prose-code:font-medium">
        <h2>Differential testing at the center</h2>
        <p>
            The primary evidence is boring in the best way: run this engine and a trusted engine on the same (pattern, input) pairs and compare everything — match, no-match, spans, every group. The suites:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6">
            <li><strong>5.7 million cases</strong> from RE2's exhaustive test suite — 0 failures.</li>
            <li><strong>~300 million fuzz cases</strong> against re2j from overnight soaks — 0 divergences. (Yes, the fuzz module has its own Gradle task; the test JVMs want 4 GB of heap.)</li>
            <li>re2j-parity suites, Glenn Fowler's <em>testregex</em> corpus, and OpenJDK's <code>java.util.regex</code> regression corpus — the last one being a festival of years of real-world parser edge cases.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The layered oracle</h2>
        <p>
            When a divergence <em>does</em> appear in development, the question is always "which layer is wrong?" — and the engine keeps a reference stack to answer it in one run:
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 p-4">
            <div class="mono space-y-1 text-[13px] text-zinc-700">
                <div>re2j 1.8                <span class="text-zinc-400">← behavioral oracle (what users expect)</span></div>
                <div>reference Pike VM       <span class="text-zinc-400">← the semantics of chapter 04, directly</span></div>
                <div>VM interpreter           <span class="text-zinc-400">← the Tdfa tables</span></div>
                <div>ASM backend              <span class="text-zinc-400">← the generated bytecode</span></div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">Any disagreement between adjacent layers pins the bug to a single layer — determinization, materialization, or codegen — without a debugger.</p>
        </div>
        <p>
            Two conformance tests guard the seams that static testing can't see: <code>StrategyConformanceTest</code> asserts the interpreter and the bytecode backend pick <em>identical strategy traces</em> (identical results could hide a different algorithm), and <code>EmittedSurfaceConformanceTest</code> asserts every <code>@EmittedSurface</code>-annotated member the generated code links by name still exists with the exact signature.
        </p>

        <h2>Performance as a test</h2>
        <p>
            Correctness has a performance shadow: <code>scripts/bench-regression.sh</code> gates merges on a 15% rule against JMH baselines, because "fast" is one of the library's actual promises. The benchmark suite covers both backends, search and anchored regimes, the ReDoS guard case, and a 110-scenario real-world corpus; headline numbers and known gaps live in <code>BENCHMARKS.md</code> — including the honest one that <code>java.util.regex</code> still wins ~2× on literal-prefixed search of medium inputs.
        </p>

        <h2>The vision: finished, then frozen</h2>
        <p>
            The README's last section is titled "Vision" and says the quiet part loud: <em>a finished library — bounded scope, all bugs fixed, then frozen. Think TeX, not a platform.</em> One algorithm (TDFA) for every pattern, no dispatch across engines, no plugin surface. The inspiration it names — Datadog's reggie — dispatches across multiple engines for peak performance; this engine chose the opposite trade: a single algorithm whose worst case you can reason about, and a site like this one that can explain <em>all of it</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you've read this far, you now know how a pattern becomes bytecode: tags and priorities in the graph, histories resolved at determinization, registers allocated like a compiler's, dead twins merged, anchors compiled into 64-way position tables, search as interned live-sets with kill points — and a budget ledger making sure none of it ever runs away. The playgrounds are all still there if you want another pass; the paper (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.01398" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Borsotti &amp; Trofimovich 2022</a>) is remarkably readable once the code has grounded it; and the source is the final authority.
        </p>
    </div>
</Base>
